11:28:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Good question! Here's what's actually going on: **Gum does have calories, even though you spit it out** The calorie count on gum packaging reflects the ingredients in the whole piece—sweeteners (sugar or sugar alcohols), softeners, and flavorings. Many of these dissolve out of the gum and into your saliva as you chew, and you swallow that saliva. So you're absorbing a portion of the calories even though the rubbery gum base itself passes through. **Why the calorie count is so low** Most gum only has 5-10 calories per piece because: - The gum base (the chewy part) is largely indigestible and isn't absorbed by your body—it just gets swallowed or spit out - Only the sweeteners and flavoring compounds actually contribute calories - Sugar-free gums use sugar alcohols (like xylitol or sorbitol) that have fewer calories than sugar and aren't fully absorbed either **The bottom line** You *are* technically consuming something—just not the whole piece of gum. The flavor and sweetness leach out into your mouth over time, and that's where the calories come from. The base itself is essentially calorie-free filler that you discard. So it's less "eating gum" and more "extracting the edible parts of gum through chewing" — similar to how you might suck the flavor out of a hard candy without swallowing something solid.
